          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The flower directly avows her loving emotional state and explains that the prince had failed to know it because of her own conduct.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not undertake a future action, obligation, or relationship. The surrounding requests that he be happy and leave, and her rejection of the globe, are separate speech acts rather than commitments encoded by “je t'aime.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The avowal occurs as a composed disclosure and explanation, not as a reflex-like affective outburst. The narration explicitly characterizes the flower's manner as calm.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Its core force is adequately represented as a direct avowal of love. Although “Mais oui” gives the avowal a corrective or revelatory role in context, that role does not constitute a substantial independent mode beyond presenting the emotional state as true.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the prince's departure farewell, after apologizing for having been foolish, the flower calmly discloses that she loves him and says that her behavior prevented him from knowing it. She is also concealing her tears and urging him to leave.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking is made by the focal expression. The flower separately urges the prince to be happy and to depart, and declines further protection under the globe. The prince's earlier surprise is described, but no explicit uptake of the love avowal is supplied.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognizable as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry's Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince). The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports the represented avowal and provides narrative evidence of affection, including concealed tears, but it does not establish broader relationship history, any future commitment arising from the avowal, or the prince's explicit response to it.\"}"
        }
